\p{The upper curve is the steady current on the whole interval, and
the lower curve is the part #{-90\le V\le -50}, where the current
changes sign. The horizontal line is #{F=0}. The drawing uses the
parameters of the [Hodgkin–Huxley system](fnip-0071).}

\p{The SNLC column of \citet{Table 3.1, p. 51}{ermentrout2010mathematical}
changes 4 entries of the Hopf column in \ref{fnip-0005}:
#{\phi=0.067}, #{g_{\mathrm{Ca}}=4}, #{V_{3}=12}, and
#{V_{4}=17.4}. The steady current of that column has two folds.
Between the folds an applied current is attained at three
voltages, and outside them at one.
\citet{p. 59}{ermentrout2010mathematical} says that there are three
equilibria for an applied current between about #{-15} and
#{+40}, and that only the lower one is stable. Figure 3.6a on
that page draws the two folds.}

